=== PARITY (web source reproduced end-to-end) ===
  Web SettingField.tsx is a PURE presentational leaf — it fetches nothing. It renders
  a <div> with a label row (mb-1.5 flex items-center gap-1) holding a <label>
  (text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-wider text-[var(--text-muted)]) + an
  optional inline <HelpIcon>, then arbitrary {children}. Reproduced:
   - <label> -> SettingFieldLabelRow: Font.TS.label (12/medium) + .textCase(.uppercase)
     + .tracking(TSTypeMetrics.labelTracking=0.60, web tracking-wider) +
     Color.TS.textMuted (web text-[var(--text-muted)]). gap-1 -> HStack TSSpacing.xs(4);
     mb-1.5 -> VStack TSSpacing.xs.
   - {children} -> generic @ViewBuilder Content, rendered verbatim under the label.
   - help && <HelpIcon> -> SettingFieldHelpButton, gated on the same condition.
   - <HelpIcon> contract (web components/ui/HelpIcon.tsx) reproduced by the pure
     SettingFieldHelpResolver:
       • text = i18nKey ? t(i18nKey,{defaultValue:content??''}) : (content??'')
       • render NOTHING when text is empty (web `if (!text) return null`)
       • aria-label = for ? t('a11y.helpFor',{field:for}) : t('help.tooltip.iconLabel')
       • aria-describedby = for ? `${for}-help` : undefined
     Native idiom: the "(?)" (SF Symbol questionmark.circle) reveals the text in a
     popover and, on macOS, a native .help() hover tooltip (web <Tooltip>); the per-
     field aria-label becomes the VoiceOver accessibilityLabel and the help text the
     accessibilityHint (the native analogue of aria-describedby). The describedByID is
     also applied as the popover body's accessibilityIdentifier for parity.
   - Field-level vs page-level: the web distinguishes <HelpIcon> (field-level, per-field
     "Help for {field}") from HelpTooltip (page-level). This surface renders its own
     field-level trigger rather than the shared page-level TSHelpTooltip so the per-field
     label + inline sizing match the source exactly.

=== I18N (P1/S10) ===
  The web SettingField extracts NO strings of its own — `label` and the help
  i18nKey/content are supplied by the parent call-site. The only keys this surface
  OWNS are the native accessibility chrome of the inline help trigger, which the web
  <HelpIcon> resolves through the global t():
    a11y.helpFor           -> "Help for %@"  (web "Help for {{field}}")
    help.tooltip.iconLabel -> "More info"
  Both are declared in SettingField.strings and referenced in SettingField.Model.swift
  (referenced == declared == 2 chrome keys). 6 settingField.preview.* keys back the
  #if DEBUG previews only. No English literals in the Swift sources (the help text and
  the labels resolve through LocalizedStringKey / the SettingFieldStrings facade).

